WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.068
Command of Evidence: Textual

00:00:03.448 --> 00:00:07.589
Split the claim into parts. Evidence must hit every part.

00:00:08.789 --> 00:00:09.880
How to spot it

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:11.937
A claim or hypothesis

00:00:12.487 --> 00:00:16.256
The question says “most directly support” or “weaken”

00:00:16.806 --> 00:00:20.195
It’s testing: what actually counts as evidence

00:00:21.395 --> 00:00:22.162
The method

00:00:22.562 --> 00:00:24.774
Step 1: Find the exact claim

00:00:25.054 --> 00:00:27.961
Usually the last sentence, or the hypothesis.

00:00:28.511 --> 00:00:30.972
Step 2: Split it into parts

00:00:31.252 --> 00:00:34.027
Toe pads + bigger + after the storm.

00:00:34.577 --> 00:00:37.065
Step 3: Support or weaken?

00:00:37.345 --> 00:00:39.529
Decide before reading the choices.

00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:42.595
Step 4: Hit every part

00:00:42.875 --> 00:00:44.683
The right topic isn’t enough.

00:00:45.233 --> 00:00:46.328
In a few words

00:00:46.478 --> 00:00:49.202
Claim. Parts. Direction.

00:00:50.402 --> 00:00:51.489
Evidence rules

00:00:51.889 --> 00:00:54.624
Support = the predicted result happens

00:00:54.904 --> 00:00:56.645
Makes the claim more likely.

00:00:57.195 --> 00:00:59.642
Weaken = same result, different cause

00:00:59.922 --> 00:01:01.957
Or the cause without the result.

00:01:02.507 --> 00:01:04.238
Hit every part of the claim

00:01:04.518 --> 00:01:06.507
Half the claim is a wrong answer.

00:01:07.057 --> 00:01:09.558
Quotes: literal meaning wins

00:01:09.838 --> 00:01:11.639
Pretty language doesn’t count.

00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:13.900
Let’s try one

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:20.633
Ecologist Amara Osei hypothesizes that a species of lizard on a small island has developed larger

00:01:20.633 --> 00:01:27.304
toe pads since a hurricane struck the island in 2017, because lizards with larger pads were better

00:01:27.304 --> 00:01:30.434
able to cling to branches during the storm’s high winds.

00:01:30.984 --> 00:01:35.532
Which finding, if true, would most directly support Osei’s hypothesis?

00:01:36.182 --> 00:01:42.610
The claim: the lizards developed larger toe pads since the 2017 hurricane.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:46.684
Two parts: bigger toe pads, and a change after the storm.

00:01:47.234 --> 00:01:52.301
Diet, other species, wind speed: none of them show that these lizards changed.

00:01:52.851 --> 00:01:57.566
B shows bigger toe pads after the hurricane. That covers both parts.

00:01:58.766 --> 00:01:59.835
Common traps

00:02:00.235 --> 00:02:01.871
Right topic, wrong claim

00:02:02.151 --> 00:02:04.323
About the subject, not the claim.

00:02:04.873 --> 00:02:06.009
Wrong direction

00:02:06.289 --> 00:02:08.094
Supports when you need weaken.

00:02:09.294 --> 00:02:11.762
Command of Evidence: Textual

00:02:12.042 --> 00:02:14.767
Claim. Parts. Direction.

00:02:15.317 --> 00:02:15.897
Spot it

00:02:16.097 --> 00:02:19.116
A claim + “most directly support / weaken”

00:02:19.666 --> 00:02:20.372
Steps

00:02:20.572 --> 00:02:22.311
1. Find the exact claim

00:02:22.591 --> 00:02:24.283
2. Split it into parts

00:02:24.563 --> 00:02:26.175
3. Support or weaken?

00:02:26.455 --> 00:02:28.725
4. Pick the one that hits every part

00:02:29.275 --> 00:02:30.000
Remember

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.934
Support = the predicted result happens

00:02:33.214 --> 00:02:36.019
Weaken = no result, or a different cause

00:02:36.299 --> 00:02:37.984
Half the claim = wrong

00:02:38.264 --> 00:02:40.874
Quotes: the literal meaning wins

00:02:41.424 --> 00:02:41.864
Trap

00:02:42.064 --> 00:02:43.700
Right topic, wrong claim

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:47.868
